    "knowledge_point": "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test",
    "problem": "For the function $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}^{″}(0)<0$\n\nII. $x=0$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n\nIII. ${f}^{″}(1)>0$\n\nIV. $x=1$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$",
    "worked_solution": "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=12x-12{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 12x(1-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=0,1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12-24x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =12>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-12<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false while statement VI is true. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only\".",
